INT. SECRETARY'S OFFICE, NEW YORK INSURANCE COMPANY - NIGHT (1927)

A cramped office after hours. A single green-shaded lamp. Filing cabinets crowd the walls like silent clerks. On the desk, a bulky wax-cylinder dictating machine, horn pointed up.

MILLICENT HARGROVE, thirties, trim in a navy dress, hair pinned firm, sits very straight. She winds the crank with a gloved hand and adjusts the horn.

MILLICENT
(brisk, to the machine)
Testing. Millicent Hargrove, Policy Division, March the ninth, nineteen twenty-seven. If this is heard, the ledger in the lower drawer is the true one.

She stops the machine. Presses a lever. The cylinder spins back, crackling.

MILLICENT (V.O.)
(from the machine, thin and tinny)
...the ledger in the lower drawer is the true one.

She nods, satisfied. Then her eyes lift to the frosted glass of the door. A shadow passes slowly. She holds perfectly still.

The shadow stops. The doorknob rattles.

MILLICENT (CONT'D)
(whispering, to herself)
Not yet. Not yet.

She snatches the cylinder from the machine and wraps it in her scarf. The knob turns again. She slides the lower drawer open, drops the bundle inside, locks it, and pockets the key in her stocking.

The door opens a crack. Lamplight catches the edge of a man's hat. Millicent lifts her chin and smiles the brightest smile of her life.

MILLICENT (CONT'D)
(calling out)
Come in, do. I've only just finished.
